<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Context adaptation automates prompt engineering in LLM-based systems by iteratively revising tunable prompts from task feedback, without modifying model weights. Extending this paradigm to multi-LLM agentic systems is crucial: existing methods suffer from inaccurate credit assignment and lack convergence guarantees. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>raph-based <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>arget <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>ack-<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>ropagation (GTBP), a context adaptation framework for agentic workflows modeled as directed acyclic graphs.
GTBP propagates local target outputs backward through the workflow graph and uses target–output discrepancies to guide a stage-wise prompt update mechanism.
Theoretically, we show that GTBP’s stage-wise prompt updates become stable over iterations, and that a sufficiently capable LLM optimizer can decrease the overall objective.
Empirically, GTBP consistently outperforms strong baselines across three benchmarks while maintaining comparable computational cost.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With input contexts such as task instructions, tool-use guidelines, and memory-retrieval
instructions that guide how external information is selected and incorporated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulhoff et al., 2024</a>)</cite>,
large language models (LLMs) can coordinate multiple LLM-powered modules in
<em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">agentic systems</em> for multi-step problem solving
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li, 2025</a>)</cite>. This paper studies <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">context adaptation</em> for such
multi-module LLM systems, where input contexts are automatically revised from
task feedback while model weights remain frozen.
Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> denote an agentic system with frozen model weights and a tunable
prompt set <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a task distribution <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and a
task-level loss function <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, context adaptation aims to find the optimal <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\Phi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\Phi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizing <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\Phi}^{*}=\argmin_{\Pi_{\Phi}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{T}}\left[\ell\!\left(\Phi(x;\Pi_{\Phi})\right)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.247em;" width="0.247em"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\Phi}^{*}=\argmin_{\Pi_{\Phi}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{T}}\left[\ell\!\left(\Phi(x;\Pi_{\Phi})\right)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Early agentic systems often rely on human-designed contexts, prompts, and instructions to coordinate multiple LLM-powered modules
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Context adaptation automates this
process by updating tunable prompts from task feedback, without modifying the model weights.
Recent advances in LLMs, including
longer context windows and lower-latency inference
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemini Team, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite>, make it increasingly feasible to
treat LLMs not only as task solvers but also as optimizers that iteratively
inspect, critique, and revise prompts. This makes
context adaptation a practical alternative to costly manual prompt engineering,
especially for workflows in multi-module agentic systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In multi-module agentic systems, context adaptation introduces
<em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">credit assignment</em>: final errors must be attributed to the
responsible module-specific contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meulemans et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
Agentic workflows involve text-based contexts, discrete actions, and
black-box LLM modules, making gradient-based credit assignment, widely used in artificial neural networks (ANNs), difficult to
apply directly.
To assign credit, methods such as GEPA and ACE employ LLM calls that take the entire agentic workflow as input
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, because this assignment is
performed implicitly rather than through an explicitly defined
inference procedure, it can be non-deterministic and unclear which prompts should be
updated. This leads to the challenge known as <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attribution ambiguity</em>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To explicitly define credit-assignment pipelines over multi-agent systems,
methods such as TextGrad <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuksekgonul et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and Agentic Neural
Networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>)</cite> model multi-agent systems with
graph structures and propagate LLM-generated textual feedback
across agent nodes. However, these pipelines remain largely heuristic and are
not directly coupled with the prompt optimization objective in
Eq. <a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Moreover, after credits are assigned,
heuristic rule-based LLM optimizer are employed to update prompts. Their convergence properties in multi-module agentic systems remain theoretically underexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we draw inspiration from Difference Target Propagation (DTP), which assigns credit by propagating local targets rather than
gradients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meulemans et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Building on this
idea, we propose <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>raph-based <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>arget
<span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>ack-<span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>ropagation (<span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GTBP</span>), a context adaptation pipeline for agentic systems.
In summary, this paper makes the following contributions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.2em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose GTBP, a graph-based credit-assignment framework for
context adaptation in multi-LLM agentic systems. Instead of relying on
trajectory-level reflection, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, GTBP follows the workflow graph to infer local
target outputs for sub-modules and uses target-output
discrepancies to guide stage-wise prompt updates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a theoretical analysis of GTBP, showing that its stage-wise prompt updates become stable over iterations and that the task-level objective defined in Eq. <a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be decreased with a sufficiently capable LLM optimizer.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically validate GTBP on real-world benchmark datasets and show
that it consistently improves prompt optimization performance compared with
strong baseline methods.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.14155v1/latex/figure/schema.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/241;" width="429" height="241" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Overview of GTBP for context adaptation. After forward execution, GTBP infers local targets for intermediate modules from the final target output. Each module then compares its current output with the inferred target, and the resulting credit signal is used to update its prompt without requiring gradient access.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Credit Assignment</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Credit assignment is a fundamental problem in learning systems, where global
feedback must be attributed to the internal components responsible for an
outcome <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Minsky, 1961</a>)</cite>. In ANNs, it is commonly
addressed through chain-rule-based gradient backpropagation
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bottou et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. Alternative methods explore different credit
assignment mechanisms beyond gradient backpropagation. For example, Decoupled
Neural Interfaces decouple credit assignment by learning synthetic gradients for
intermediate modules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaderberg et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. Difference Target
Propagation (DTP), in contrast, offers a target-based alternative by assigning
desired outputs to intermediate components <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio, 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meulemans et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With rapid advances in LLM context length <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and inference efficiency
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, methods such as MIPROv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Opsahl-Ong et al. (2024)</a></cite>, GEPA
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and ACE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> use LLM reasoning
to approximate credit assignment over textual execution traces. However, because credit is assigned through black-box LLM-based reflection, the process remains difficult to control. To make it more structured, recent work such as TextGrad <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuksekgonul et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and Agentic Neural Networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>)</cite> propagates textual gradients through neural-network-like agentic workflows. TextResNet instead improves attribution through a credit-assignment-friendly residual architecture for signal decomposition and routing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Context Adaptation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Context adaptation extends manual prompt engineering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulhoff et al., 2024</a>)</cite> by automatically revising input contexts, such as prompts, instructions, or retrieved evidence, using task feedback or LLM-generated feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Representative methods differ in how they update textual contexts.
Early automatic prompt optimization methods rely on non-textual feedback, such as task-level scores. APE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and OPRO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> search over LLM-generated prompt candidates using scalar evaluation signals. Moving beyond metric-based signals, recent studies such as Reflexion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and GEPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal et al., 2025</a>)</cite> exploit textual feedback for iterative improvement. With textual credit signals, prompts can be updated in more structured ways, such as playbooks in ACE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The theoretical understanding of context adaptation is still emerging. Since modern LLMs are largely built on Transformer architectures, early work relates context adaptation to weight updates, showing that prompt updating can induce effects equivalent to parameter updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Recent work further suggests that context-conditioned behavior may be related to implicit optimization over model weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dherin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, although a general theoretical characterization of this relationship remains open.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Problem Formulation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we instantiate the objective in
Eq. <a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> by defining the agentic system as a
directed graph, and specifying the workflow studied in this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An agentic workflow is modeled as a directed acyclic graph
<math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where each node <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a LLM-invoking
submodule</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{v}=M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{v}=M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">parameterized by a tunable prompt <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and input <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any node <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, the input is formed by aggregating
the outputs of its predecessor nodes</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}=\{\,O_{u}\mid(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}\,\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}=\{\,O_{u}\mid(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}\,\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">In this graph-based workflow, node outputs are passed to downstream nodes and
aggregated to form subsequent inputs. Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> describes how the workflow executes the graph and combines intermediate nodes’ outputs into the final output.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Forward Execution of a Graph-based Agentic Workflow</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
            
            
          Graph <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math> with input-node set
<math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> and output node <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\mathrm{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mathrm{out}}</annotation></semantics></math>, external input
<math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, prompts <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\pi_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pi_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
            
            
          Final system output <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each node <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> in topological order <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
            
            
             <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}\leftarrow x_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}\leftarrow x_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
            
            
             <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}\leftarrow\{\,O_{u}\mid(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}\,\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}\leftarrow\{\,O_{u}\mid(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}\,\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{v}\leftarrow M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{v}\leftarrow M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\leftarrow O_{v_{\mathrm{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\leftarrow O_{v_{\mathrm{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we focus on a one-hidden-layer agentic workflow. Given an input
message <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the workflow contains <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> prompt-tunable LLM submodules that
produce intermediate responses</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="O_{k}=M_{k}(x;\pi_{k}),\qquad k=1,\dots,K." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{k}=M_{k}(x;\pi_{k}),\qquad k=1,\dots,K.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Given these intermediate responses, the workflow contains an output node that aggregates them with the original input to produce the final output:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=M_{\mathrm{out}}(x,O_{1},\dots,O_{K};\pi_{\mathrm{out}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=M_{\mathrm{out}}(x,O_{1},\dots,O_{K};\pi_{\mathrm{out}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this paper, all LLM parameters are fixed, and only the prompts
<math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\pi_{1},\dots,\pi_{K},\pi_{\mathrm{out}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pi_{1},\dots,\pi_{K},\pi_{\mathrm{out}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated. This structure creates a
simple credit-assignment setting where the final error may arise from any
intermediate module or from the output module.
Given this workflow graph, let
<math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\Phi}=\{\pi_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\Phi}=\{\pi_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the prompt set of the overall
system. For a textual input <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, graph execution induces the final system output
<math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\Phi(x;\Pi_{\Phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\Phi(x;\Pi_{\Phi})</annotation></semantics></math> following Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. For an
input–reference pair <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from the task distribution
<math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(y,y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(y,y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> denote an output-level loss measuring the
discrepancy between the system output and the reference. The context adaptation
objective is then</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\Phi}^{*}=\argmin_{\Pi_{\Phi}}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{T}}\left[\ell\!\left(\Phi(x;\Pi_{\Phi}),y^{*}\right)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.247em;" width="0.247em"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\Phi}^{*}=\argmin_{\Pi_{\Phi}}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{T}}\left[\ell\!\left(\Phi(x;\Pi_{\Phi}),y^{*}\right)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is instantiated using the evaluation metric
associated with each dataset <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Proposed Method</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">G</span>raph-based
<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">T</span>arget <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">B</span>ack-<span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">P</span>ropagation
(<span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GTBP</span>), which uses LLM-guided target propagation to trace
output-level feedback backward through the agentic workflow and assign
localized optimization signals to prompt-tunable sub-modules. We then introduce
a stage-wise mechanism for updating the corresponding prompts. An overview of GTBP is included in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>LLM-Guided Target Propagation</h3>

<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Target Propagation in GTBP</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            Graph <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math>, cached node outputs
<math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{O_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{O_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>, output target
<math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v_{\mathrm{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v_{\mathrm{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, local contexts <math id="alg2.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
            Local targets <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{O}_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{O}_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each node <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> in reverse topological order <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
              Compute <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. <a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each node <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="alg2.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
               Get <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg2.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{O}_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{O}_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the graph-based workflow, GTBP adapts the idea of target
propagation to assign credit to LLM-based modules in agentic workflows. Given an input–reference pair
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y^{*})\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y^{*})\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math> produces node outputs
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{O_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{O_{v}\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> including the final output <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{v_{\mathrm{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{v_{\mathrm{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math> following Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Let the expected output for each node <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. Target propagation starts by assigning the reference output <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as the target of the
final output <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v_{\mathrm{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v_{\mathrm{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The goal is then to propagate this output-level target backward through the
graph and infer local targets for the other nodes. For a node <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, its output
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> may be consumed by multiple successor nodes. For each successor node
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, GTBP infers a target input for node <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>
by solving</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\argmin_{I}\left[\ell\left(M_{v}\!\left(I;\pi_{v}\right),\hat{O}_{v}\right)+\lambda\ell(I,I_{v})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmin_{I}\left[\ell\left(M_{v}\!\left(I;\pi_{v}\right),\hat{O}_{v}\right)+\lambda\ell(I,I_{v})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the original input to node <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>
denotes the inferred target input for node <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. The first term encourages
node <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to produce target outputs for its predecessor nodes <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the second term
regularizes the inferred target input to remain close to the original input
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. The coefficient <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controls this locality constraint.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In a general directed graph, a node may branch to multiple successors, which can lead to multiple backward-inferred targets for the same node. This ambiguity does not occur in our one-hidden-layer workflow. As defined in Eqs. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, each intermediate node connects only to the output node, and therefore receives a single target.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, Eq. <a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is generally not analytically solvable
in LLM-based agentic systems, since sub-module <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> might be a black-box LLM
call, and inputs and outputs within the system are text-based and non-differentiable. To address this problem, GTBP
approximates the target output using an LLM-guided backward
operator:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{v}=\mathrm{LLM}\!\left(P_{\mathrm{back}}(\hat{O}_{v},I_{v},c_{v})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.270em;" width="2.270em"><mi>LLM</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>back</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{v}=\mathrm{LLM}\!\left(P_{\mathrm{back}}(\hat{O}_{v},I_{v},c_{v})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{back}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>back</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{back}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a backward inference prompt template and
<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the local context of node <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, including its role description,
system prompt, and task constraints. A concrete example of such a backward inference prompt is shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S4.F2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">

<span id="S4.F2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="width:324.3pt;">
<span id="S4.F2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt template <math id="S4.F2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{back}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>back</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{back}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S4.F2.2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span>
system prompt of node <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>;
original input of node <math id="S4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>;
target output of node <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="S4.F2.2.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instruction:</span>
Generate a target input <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> for node <math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> such that node <math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is more
likely to produce the target output <math id="S4.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> when processing <math id="S4.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> with
its current prompt. The generated target input should remain close in meaning
and structure to the original input <math id="S4.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Backward target-inference prompt template used to infer the target
input for a downstream node.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, by applying Eq. <a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> from the output node to earlier
nodes in reverse topological order, GTBP generates targets for each sub-module node in the agentic workflow.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> Prompt Update for Tunable Prompts</figcaption>
<div id="alg3.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            Node <math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, current prompt
<math id="alg3.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}=\langle c_{v,1},\dots,c_{v,m}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}=\langle c_{v,1},\dots,c_{v,m}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>,
module-level update set
<math id="alg3.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{v}=\{(I_{v}^{(i)},O_{v}^{(i)},\hat{O}_{v}^{(i)})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{v}=\{(I_{v}^{(i)},O_{v}^{(i)},\hat{O}_{v}^{(i)})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>,
update budget <math id="alg3.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, claim-addition marker <math id="alg3.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{v}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{v}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
            Updated prompt <math id="alg3.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
            Compute discrepancies
<math id="alg3.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Delta(O_{v}^{(i)},\hat{O}_{v}^{(i)})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Delta(O_{v}^{(i)},\hat{O}_{v}^{(i)})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg3.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{v}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{v}=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              Add one new prompt claim to <math id="alg3.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
              Set <math id="alg3.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leftarrow n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leftarrow n-1</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg3.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
            Select at most <math id="alg3.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> existing prompt entries from
<math id="alg3.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle c_{v,1},\dots,c_{v,m}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle c_{v,1},\dots,c_{v,m}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> for revision

</div>
<div id="alg3.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
            Update the selected prompt entries in <math id="alg3.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. <a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.2 Stage-wise Prompt Updating ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> with
<math id="alg3.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and discrepancies
<math id="alg3.l9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> Updated <math id="alg3.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Stage-wise Prompt Updating</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before introducing the proposed prompt update pipeline, we first specify the structure of the tunable prompt. The prompt associated with LLM module <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in the agentic workflow is formatted as an
ordered list of textual claims <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}=\langle c_{v,1},\dots,c_{v,m}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}=\langle c_{v,1},\dots,c_{v,m}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of prompt claims.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y^{*})\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y^{*})\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, the target output <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> for each
sub-module <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in the agentic workflow <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained by the
target propagation procedure described in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Given the input <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and the target output <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sub-module-level
prompt update for node <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}^{\mathrm{opt}}:=\argmin_{\pi_{v}}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{T}}\;\ell\!\left(M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v}),\hat{O}_{v}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>opt</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.247em;" width="0.247em"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}^{\mathrm{opt}}:=\argmin_{\pi_{v}}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{T}}\;\ell\!\left(M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v}),\hat{O}_{v}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In practice, we approximate this objective using an LLM-based optimizer on a
mini-batch sampled from the task distribution:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}^{\mathrm{LLM}}:=\mathrm{LLM}\!\left(P^{m,n}_{\mathrm{optimize}}(\pi_{v},\mathcal{S}_{v}(\mathcal{B}))\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.270em;" width="2.270em"><mi>LLM</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>optimize</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}^{\mathrm{LLM}}:=\mathrm{LLM}\!\left(P^{m,n}_{\mathrm{optimize}}(\pi_{v},\mathcal{S}_{v}(\mathcal{B}))\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a finite mini-batch sampled from
the task distribution,
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{v}(\mathcal{B})=\{(I_{v}^{(i)},O_{v}^{(i)},\hat{O}_{v}^{(i)})\}_{i\in\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{v}(\mathcal{B})=\{(I_{v}^{(i)},O_{v}^{(i)},\hat{O}_{v}^{(i)})\}_{i\in\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the module-level update set for node <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{optimize}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>optimize</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{optimize}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the prompt optimization template for the LLM.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S4.F3.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">

<span id="S4.F3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="width:324.3pt;">
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt template <math id="S4.F3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{optimize}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>optimize</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{optimize}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span>
current prompt <math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>; local update set <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{v}(\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{v}(\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>;
edit budget <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>; claim-addition marker <math id="S4.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{v}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{v}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instruction:</span>
Given the examples in <math id="S4.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{v}(\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{v}(\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>, revise the current prompt
<math id="S4.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> so that module <math id="S4.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is more likely to produce outputs close to the
target outputs <math id="S4.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> for inputs <math id="S4.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Use the current outputs <math id="S4.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{v}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{v}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> to identify the gap between current and
desired behavior. If <math id="S4.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{v}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{v}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, add one new claim to the prompt and use one unit
of the edit budget. Then update no more than <math id="S4.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-a_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-a_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> existing claims.</span>
</span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Prompt optimization template used by the stage-wise LLM optimizer.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Graph-based Target Back-Propogation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Algorithm <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ 4.3 Graph-based Target Back-Propogation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, GTBP performs context adaptation by repeatedly applying LLM-guided target propagation to mini-batches sampled from the task distribution and updating the tunable prompts accordingly. At each
iteration, a finite mini-batch
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\{(x^{(i)},y^{*(i)})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\{(x^{(i)},y^{*(i)})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from the data distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each example, the agentic workflow is executed in the to cache node-level inputs and outputs following Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Then the local targets for sub-modules of the agentic workflow are inferred following Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Finally, the inputs, cached outputs, and inferred targets are collected across the mini-batch and used to update the tunable prompts following Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">GTBP further controls prompt updates through two stage-wise scheduling
parameters. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the initial edit budget, i.e., the initial
number of prompt claims allowed to be updated. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
claim-addition interval, meaning that a new prompt claim is added after every
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the edit-budget decay interval: after every <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>
iterations, the number of editable prompt claims is reduced by one, but never
below one.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg4" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 4</span> </span> Graph-based Target Propagation</figcaption>
<div id="alg4.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg4.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            Task distribution <math id="alg4.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, iterations <math id="alg4.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="alg4.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>,
workflow <math id="alg4.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, tunable nodes <math id="alg4.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>,
initial prompts <math id="alg4.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\Phi}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\Phi}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>, initial edit budget <math id="alg4.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
claim-addition interval <math id="alg4.l1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, edit-budget decay interval <math id="alg4.l1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
            Updated prompts <math id="alg4.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\Phi}^{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\Phi}^{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg4.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg4.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg4.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
              Sample mini-batch <math id="alg4.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{t}\sim\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{t}\sim\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg4.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg4.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{(i)},y^{*(i)})\in\mathcal{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{(i)},y^{*(i)})\in\mathcal{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg4.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
               Run forward pass on <math id="alg4.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> by Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
               Cache the corresponding <math id="alg4.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(I_{v}^{(i)},O_{v}^{(i)})\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(I_{v}^{(i)},O_{v}^{(i)})\}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
               Set <math id="alg4.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v_{\mathrm{out}}}^{(i)}\leftarrow y^{*(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v_{\mathrm{out}}}^{(i)}\leftarrow y^{*(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
               Infer local targets by Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg4.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg4.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg4.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
              
              
              Set edit budget <math id="alg4.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> according to <math id="alg4.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> and claim-addition marker <math id="alg4.l11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}\leftarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}\leftarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="alg4.l11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is divisible by <math id="alg4.l11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg4.l11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise

</div>
<div id="alg4.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg4.l12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg4.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg4.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">13:</span>
              
              
               Update <math id="alg4.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg4.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> with edit budget <math id="alg4.l13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and
claim-addition marker <math id="alg4.l13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">14:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg4.l14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg4.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg4.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">15:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg4.l15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg4.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg4.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">16:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg4.l16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg4.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\Phi}^{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\Phi}^{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theoretical analysis of GTBP is challenging because practical LLM-based
agent modules are deep, sequence-level, and text-based. Therefore, we first
introduce a simplified theoretical setting. Under this setting, we analyze two
properties. First, the change of the overall objective in
Eq. <a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> converges to zero as the number of
iterations increases. Second, the prompt-update pipeline induces an
optimization effect similar to gradient descent on trainable weights.
Together, these results explain the stability and descent behavior of the
proposed method. For readability, proofs are deferred to Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Appendix of Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Simplified Theoretical Setting</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-token setting.</span>
We restrict the input-reference pair <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to a single-token setting. This avoids sequence-level notation
and makes the following loss and stability analysis easier to state.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt-input concatenation.</span>
For each node <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in the agentic workflow <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume that the node input <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and prompt <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are directly concatenated before being processed by the LLM module, without applying an additional prompt
template.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-block module approximation.</span>
We view each LLM module as a single transformer block:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v})=H_{W_{v}}\!\left(A_{v}(I_{v},\pi_{v})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v})=H_{W_{v}}\!\left(A_{v}(I_{v},\pi_{v})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> maps the concatenated input and prompt to a contextual hidden
representation, and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{W_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{W_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the linear transformation with weight <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Stability of Prompt Updates</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The convergence analysis builds on the implicit weight theorem in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dherin et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which shows that changing the input text of a
transformer block can be equivalently represented as a weight update without input text changes.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Implicit Weight)<span id="Thmlemma1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a transformer block with frozen parameters, let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> denote the update
iteration of GTBP. For a fixed node input <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, changing the prompt from
<math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as inducing an
input-dependent effective inference-time weight update
<math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{v,I_{v}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{v,I_{v}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{v,W_{v}}\!\left(I_{v};\pi_{v}^{(t)}\right)=M_{v,W_{v}+\Delta W_{v,I_{v}}^{(t)}}\!\left(I_{v};\pi_{v}^{(t-1)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{v,W_{v}}\!\left(I_{v};\pi_{v}^{(t)}\right)=M_{v,W_{v}+\Delta W_{v,I_{v}}^{(t)}}\!\left(I_{v};\pi_{v}^{(t-1)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">To save space, we present a simplified version of
Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 (Implicit Weight). ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in the main text, while the full statement is
provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Full version of Lemma ‣ Appendix A Appendix of Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>. The full version is used in the following proofs of theorems, but
is not needed for the main statement.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by Theorem 3.2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023)</a></cite>, the effect of a
single prompt perturbation decreases inversely with prompt length. Since GTBP
accumulates prompt claims over iterations and eventually updates only one
claim at a time, we abstract this behavior into the following stability assumption to upper bound the implicit weight update.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Prompt-length stability)<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each node <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in the agentic workflow <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, the induced change in the contextual representation satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|A_{v}(I_{v},\pi_{v}^{(t)})-A_{v}(I_{v},\pi_{v}^{(t-1)})\right\|_{2}=\mathcal{O}(t^{-1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|A_{v}(I_{v},\pi_{v}^{(t)})-A_{v}(I_{v},\pi_{v}^{(t-1)})\right\|_{2}=\mathcal{O}(t^{-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we show that, under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the implicit weight increment <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{v,I_{v}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{v,I_{v}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> whose explicit form is given in Eq. <a href="#A1.E10" title="In Lemma . ‣ A.1 Full version of Lemma ‣ Appendix A Appendix of Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> can be upper bounded in terms of the update iteration <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in GTBP.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{v}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{v}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A_{v}(I_{v},\pi_{v}^{(t-1)})\|_{2}\geq\gamma_{v}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A_{v}(I_{v},\pi_{v}^{(t-1)})\|_{2}\geq\gamma_{v}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Prompt-length stability). ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the implicit weight update in Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 (Implicit Weight). ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be bounded by</span></p>
<table id="S5.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Delta W_{v,I_{v}}^{(t)}\|_{F}=\mathcal{O}\!\left(t^{-1}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.654em;" width="0.654em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Delta W_{v,I_{v}}^{(t)}\|_{F}=\mathcal{O}\!\left(t^{-1}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that the implicit weight update
induced by a stage-wise prompt update becomes smaller as the iteration number
increases. To connect this update stability with the corresponding local objective in Eq. <a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.2 Stage-wise Prompt Updating ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we show in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> that the change of the objective is also
upper bounded.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Per-sample regularity)<span id="Thmassumption3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The per-sample local target loss <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\!\left(M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v}^{(t)}),\hat{O}_{v}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.247em;" width="0.247em"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\!\left(M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v}^{(t)}),\hat{O}_{v}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. <a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.2 Stage-wise Prompt Updating ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> is second-order smooth around <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, and its gradient
with respect to <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is also bounded along the path from <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{v}+\Delta W_{v,I_{v}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{v}+\Delta W_{v,I_{v}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Prompt-length stability). ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Per-sample regularity). ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>,
if GTBP updates the prompt from <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the objective change can be upper bounded by</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|F_{v}(\pi^{(t)}_{v})-F_{v}(\pi^{(t-1)}_{v})|=\mathcal{O}(t^{-1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|F_{v}(\pi^{(t)}_{v})-F_{v}(\pi^{(t-1)}_{v})|=\mathcal{O}(t^{-1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{v}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{v}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the local target objective in Eq. <a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.2 Stage-wise Prompt Updating ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{v}(\pi_{v})=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{T}}\;\ell\!\left(M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v}),\hat{O}_{v}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.247em;" width="0.247em"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{v}(\pi_{v})=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{T}}\;\ell\!\left(M_{v}(I_{v};\pi_{v}),\hat{O}_{v}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Gradient-Aligned Implicit Updates</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that the local target
optimization updates in GTBP become stable as training proceeds, but stability
alone does not explain why and how the objective can decrease. Therefore, this
subsection studies whether the LLM optimizer can decrease the objective in Eq. <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> sufficiently well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">GTBP uses LLMs for both backward target inference and prompt updates. Since we
focus here on the effect of prompt updates on the objective, we simplify the
analysis by assuming that the backward operator recovers local targets for
upstream modules, as stated in
Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4. ‣ 5.3 Gradient-Aligned Implicit Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4</span></span><span id="Thmassumption4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each edge <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, the local target inference problem in
Eq. <a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.1 LLM-Guided Target Propagation ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> can be approximately solved by the
LLM-guided backward operator, i.e., with LLM-inferred target output <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{v}=M_{v}\!\big(I_{v}^{u\leftarrow\hat{O}_{u}};\pi_{v}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{v}=M_{v}\!\big(I_{v}^{u\leftarrow\hat{O}_{u}};\pi_{v}\big)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4. ‣ 5.3 Gradient-Aligned Implicit Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> simplifies the backward step by assuming that the LLM can infer local targets consistent with the downstream target. The remaining question is whether the prompt-update step can
solve the local target optimization problem well enough to reduce the overall objective. To prove this, we define the LLM’s optimization capability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt},v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>opt</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt},v}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the token-level edit distance between <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}^{\mathrm{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}^{\mathrm{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Eq. <a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.2 Stage-wise Prompt Updating ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}^{\mathrm{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>opt</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}^{\mathrm{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Eq. <a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.2 Stage-wise Prompt Updating ‣ 4 Proposed Method ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
A smaller <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt},v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>opt</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt},v}</annotation></semantics></math> means that the LLM is more capable of generating the prompt that optimizes the local target objective. Under this
condition, we can connect the local target optimization with a descent direction of the overall objective in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 5.3 Gradient-Aligned Implicit Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Prompt-length stability). ‣ 5.2 Stability of Prompt Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4. ‣ 5.3 Gradient-Aligned Implicit Updates ‣ 5 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Graph-based Target Back-Propagation for Context Adaptation in Multi-LLM Agentic Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, and for an LLM with sufficiently small <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt},v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>opt</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt},v}</annotation></semantics></math>, the overall loss gap <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big|\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{T}}[\ell(\Phi(x;\Pi_{\Phi}^{(t)}),y^{*})]-\min_{\Pi_{\Phi}}{\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{T}}\left[\ell\!\left(\Phi(x;\Pi_{\Phi}),y^{*}\right)\right]}\big|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.247em;" width="0.247em"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big|\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{T}}[\ell(\Phi(x;\Pi_{\Phi}^{(t)}),y^{*})]-\min_{\Pi_{\Phi}}{\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{T}}\left[\ell\!\left(\Phi(x;\Pi_{\Phi}),y^{*}\right)\right]}\big|</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(t^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(t^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Datasets and Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate GTBP on three real-world datasets: SubPOP, HotpotQA, and LiveBench-Math.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SubPOP</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suh et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
SubPOP evaluates whether language models can predict survey response
distributions across population subgroups. Each instance consists of a survey
question, a target subgroup, and the corresponding ground-truth response
distribution. The dataset contains 3,229 training and 133 test questions, each
paired with 22 subgroups. We use Wasserstein distance (WD) to compare predicted
and ground-truth distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HotpotQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2018)</a></cite>.
HotpotQA evaluates multi-hop question answering, where models must combine
evidence from multiple supporting documents to answer a question. We use the <em id="S6.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distractor</em> setting, in which each instance consists of a question, 2 gold paragraphs containing supporting facts, 8 distractor paragraphs, and a ground-truth answer. We randomly sample 700 questions from the training split for prompt adaptation and 1,000 questions from the validation split for evaluation. Following prior work, we report answer-level F1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LiveBench-Math</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">White et al. (2025)</a></cite>. LiveBench is a popular benchmark with objectively verifiable answers and deterministic graders. Its math subset, LiveBench-Math, contains high-school math-competition problems, symbolic-math problems, and olympiad proof-completion tasks. From the July 30, 2025 snapshot, we shuffle and split the questions into train, validation, and test sets. During evaluation, each held-out question is scored with LiveBench grader, and we report accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Baselines and Hyperparameter Settings</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare GTBP with three baseline methods under the same backbone model, GPT-4.1-mini:
<span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GTBP (proposed).</span>
Prompts in GTBP are automatically initialized from data rather than manual design. For each intermediate module <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we independently sample a random mini-batch and use an LLM to summarize its task patterns, reasoning skills, and output requirements from a distinct perspective. The resulting summary is used to initialize the prompt <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of module <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then summarize the first-layer prompts <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\pi_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pi_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate the output-module prompt <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{out}}</annotation></semantics></math>, instructing the output module to integrate the intermediate responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We instantiate GTBP with the described workflow and tune the number of intermediate modules over <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\{1,2,4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\{1,2,4\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The mini-batch size and the number of iterations are both set as 15. For prompt update, the initial number of claims in each
prompt is set to 10, and both <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> are set to 4.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GEPA.</span> We run GEPA using the DSPy implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khattab et al. (2024)</a></cite> with Pareto-based candidate selection and update skipping on perfect-scoring examples. For HotpotQA, we directly configure the optimizer hyperparameters with a reflection mini-batch size of 3, a metric-call budget of 1000.
For SubPOP, we use the <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">light</span> auto configuration with a reflection mini-batch size of 15, round-robin component selection, a merge phase with up to 5 merge invocations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero- and Few-shot.</span>
Zero-shot directly queries LLM with the task input. Few-shot adds randomly sampled training examples before the test query as
in-context demonstrations. For HotpotQA and LiveBench-Math, where inputs are
longer, we tune the number of examples over <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,3,5,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,3,5,10\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For SubPOP, we maintain consistency as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suh et al. (2025)</a></cite> and keep the number of examples as 5.</p>
</div>
